
Method:1 : Connect using username and password
==============================================

Security recommendation : 
=======================
For EC2 instances, SSH key authentication is the recommended and more secure method. 
Password authentication is generally disabled by default because it is more vulnerable to brute-force attacks
If you enable password authentication, it's a good idea to restrict SSH access in your security group to only your own public IP address instead of allowing access from everywhere (0.0.0.0/0).


# create a user
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash viswa   # -m --> to create home directory, -s /bin/bash → set login shell


# Edit the SSH configuration:
sudo vi /etc/ssh/sshd_config


# Ensure these settings are present:
PasswordAuthentication yes
PubkeyAuthentication yes


# Restart the SSH service:
sudo systemctl restart sshd


# Make sure the viswa account has a password:
sudo passwd viswa



#To Connect:
===========
ssh viswa@54.210.100.25

# When prompted: viswa@54.210.100.25's password: Enter the password (it won't be displayed as you type) and press Enter.


whoami   --> to verify the user


To disconnect:
==============
exit  or  ctrl+D   


To delete user
==============
sudo userdel john        #  --> user home directory won't be delete
sudo userdel -r john     # -r: it removes the user's home directory and its contents.


